  "title": "Money, possessions and generosity",
  "concepts": [
   "Gift",
   "Tithe",
   "Treasure",
   "Need"
  ],
  "motto": [
   "Everything comes from You.",
   "From Your hand we give."
  ],
  "lead": [
   "The Bible says more about money than many readers expect, and much of it is a promise or a warning spoken to someone in particular. This study teaches one habit for every passage about money. Before you claim a promise, read the address on it: who it was first spoken to, under which of God’s promises, and what it was for."
  ],
  "frame": [
   "Money is one of the places where the Bible is most often quoted out of its setting. A sentence spoken to the nation of Israel about rain on its fields is printed on an envelope for an offering. A proverb about how life usually goes is sold as a guarantee. A parable about a man who used money to prepare for his future is skipped because one of its characters is dishonest. So this study reads each passage with its address on it. It asks who first heard it, under which of God’s binding promises (the Bible calls such a promise a ",
   {
    "i": "covenant"
   },
   "), and what it was meant to do. Then it asks what that passage, read honestly, still says to you."
  ],
  "selection": [
   "This is a selection, not a census. These six passages were chosen because each is a main reading on money that no other study on this site walks through: two from the law of Moses, one prayer of King David, one prophet, one chapter of Luke’s Gospel and one short scene in Mark. Other passages on money belong to other studies, and they are quoted here only briefly with a link: the rich man who asked Jesus about eternal life, Jesus’ parable of the bigger barns, Paul’s collection for the poor and his charge to the rich. This study reads Scripture. It is not financial, legal or medical advice."
  ],
  "close": {
   "title": "Reading the address",
   "way": [
    "Here is the habit to carry to the next passage about money. Read the address first: who heard it, under which of God’s covenants, and what it was for. Then ask what it says about God, who owns everything and gives freely. Then ask what it says about your heart, which forgets in plenty and fears in want. Then notice what it does not promise. And then go back and read it again."
   ],
   "close": [
    "David prayed, “from Your own hand we have given to You” (",
    {
     "r": "1CH.29.14",
     "t": "1 Chronicles 29:14"
    },
    "). That is true of every gift, because it is true of the greatest gift. Paul grounds all Christian giving in what Jesus gave: “though He was rich, yet for your sakes He became poor, so that you through His poverty might become rich” (",
    {
     "r": "2CO.8.9",
     "t": "2 Corinthians 8:9"
    },
    "). ",
    {
     "i": "Grace"
    },
    ", the Bible’s word for God’s undeserved kindness, comes first, and generosity is the answer it calls out."
   ],
   "invite": [
    "If you have not yet trusted Christ, none of this begins with giving anything. Nothing you give can buy God’s favour, and you do not need to buy it. Turn from your sin and trust Jesus as Lord, who died and rose again. Read ",
    {
     "s": "../the-good-news-directions/",
     "t": "The good news: how a person is made right with God"
    },
    "."
   ],
   "question": [
    "What do you hold as if it were yours, and what would change if you held it as entrusted?"
   ],
   "again": [
    "Read the passage again before you answer."
   ],
   "passage": "1CH.29.10-19"
  }
